[FINAL-TEXT] The evidence is complete. Every BGP session across all five routers shows `Connections established: 1, dropped: 0` with zero Notifications. No session has ever bounced. The sole unusual metric — `spine_router_0_0` accepting 0 prefixes from the super_spine — is a textbook consequence of standard AS-PATH loop prevention, not a fault.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
There is no fault in this network. A thorough inspection of every BGP session in the dc_clos_bgp fabric found all eight eBGP adjacencies in the Established state with identical "Connections established: 1; dropped: 0" counters and zero BGP Notification messages sent or received on any device. All sessions have been continuously up since lab boot with default 180 s / 60 s hold/keepalive timers. Reachability from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is 100 % (10/10 packets delivered, 0 % loss). The team's claim of "BGP session flapping across the fabric" is a false premise.

The one metric that might have triggered the alarm — spine_router_0_0 accepting 0 prefixes from super_spine_router_0 — is completely normal BGP behavior, not a misconfiguration. The super_spine's BGP best-path selection uses router-ID as its final tiebreaker: both 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 paths arrive equally from spine_router_0_0 (router-ID 172.16.0.1) and spine_router_0_1 (router-ID 172.16.0.3); the lower ID wins for both prefixes. When the super_spine re-advertises those routes back to spine_router_0_0, their AS-PATHs contain AS 65100 — spine_router_0_0's own AS — so it correctly drops them as BGP AS-PATH loops. spine_router_0_1 receives both routes cleanly (its AS 65101 is absent from those paths). This asymmetry is harmless: spine_router_0_0 already holds routes to both prefixes learned directly from the two leaf routers, and both leaf routers maintain redundant paths to the remote prefix via both spines. No device needs to be restarted and no configuration change is required.
=== END ===